/-!
# Separating strict transforms and closures by an admissible blowup

Let `S` be a scheme, let `X` be an algebraic space over `S` and let `U ⊆ X` be an open subspace.
Stacks 0F46 asserts:

1. if `Z₁, Z₂ ⊆ X` are closed subspaces of finite presentation with `Z₁ ∩ Z₂ ∩ U = ∅`, then
   there is a `U`-admissible blowup `X' ⟶ X` in which the strict transforms of `Z₁` and `Z₂`
   are disjoint;
2. if `X` is quasi-compact and quasi-separated, `U` is quasi-compact and `T₁, T₂ ⊆ |U|` are
   disjoint constructible closed subsets, then there is a `U`-admissible blowup `X' ⟶ X` in
   which the closures of `T₁` and `T₂` are disjoint.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_isAdmissibleBlowup_disjoint_strictTransformPointSet`:
  Stacks 0F46, part (1).
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_isAdmissibleBlowup_disjoint_closure`: Stacks 0F46, part (2).
* `Space.exists_isOpen_inter_subspacePointSet_subset_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor_inf`:
  two closed subspaces meeting in an effective Cartier divisor are, near every point, one of
  them contained in the other.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.disjoint_subspacePointSet_of_definingIdealSheaf_eq_supportedSections`:
  a closed subspace cut out by the sections supported on `T` has no points in an open subset
  of `T`.

## Implementation notes

Part (1) is proved by blowing up `X` in the scheme-theoretic intersection `Y = Z₁ ∩ Z₂`, which
is of finite presentation and misses `U`; the source instead blows up the closed subspace
`V(𝓘₁𝓘₂)` with the help of Stacks 086F and transports the result by Stacks 0864.  For the blowup
`b` in `Y` the exceptional divisor `b⁻¹Z₁ ∩ b⁻¹Z₂` is an effective Cartier divisor.  On an affine
étale chart where it is cut out by a nonzerodivisor `f`, the ideals `𝓙ᵢ` of `b⁻¹Zᵢ` satisfy
`𝓙₁ + 𝓙₂ = (f)`, so `𝓙ᵢ = f𝓚ᵢ` with `𝓚₁ + 𝓚₂ = 𝒪`; where `𝓚ᵢ` is the unit ideal, `b⁻¹Zᵢ` lies
in the exceptional divisor, and the strict transform of `Zᵢ` — cut out by the sections supported
on the exceptional divisor — is empty there.  The quasi-compactness and quasi-separatedness
assumptions of the source are not needed for part (1) and are omitted from it.

The strict transform is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.strictTransform` of Stacks 0862, a closed
subspace of `Zᵢ ×_X X'`, taken relative to the exceptional divisor `b⁻¹Y`; since it depends on
the centre, the centre is part of the conclusion of part (1).  "The strict transforms are
disjoint" is disjointness of their sets of points in `|X'|`,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.strictTransformPointSet`.

In part (2) the closure of `Tᵢ` in `|X'|` is the closure of the set of points of `X'` mapping
into the image of `Tᵢ` in `|X|`.  The closed subspaces to which part (1) is applied are produced
directly in `X` from Stacks 0855, as complements of the quasi-compact opens `U ∖ Tᵢ`, rather
than on `U` followed by an extension by Stacks 0853.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F46](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F46)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F3V](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F3V)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0855](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0855)
